      "problem": "Find $\\dfrac {\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x\\partial y}$ given that $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} =  \\dfrac {2y} {x + y^2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\dfrac {\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x\\partial y},$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2y}{x+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({(x+{y}^{2})}^{-1}) \\\\  & =-2y(x+{y}^{2})^{-2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
